# DTF tier manifest: rpm-p3-curation  (#2358 / 1.7.0 RPM curation Phase-3)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. Curated snapshot versions + signed,
# immutable @N publication serving is DB + storage-side and storage-agnostic.
#
# The feature (#2358): an approved RPM curation set is frozen into a monotonic
# `repository_version`, PUBLISHED as AK-signed repodata, and served under
# `/rpm/{key}/@N/`. Once published the snapshot is IMMUTABLE (re-publish is
# refused; the served metadata is a frozen blob, not re-generated from the live
# approved set) and its repomd.xml.asc is REAL OpenPGP that verifies over the
# served repomd.xml under the served repomd.xml.key.
#
# Oracle (discriminating):
#   (CREATE+PUBLISH) admin POST /curation/repos/{key}/versions -> 201, then
#                    .../versions/{n}/publish -> 200. On a pre-#2358 image the
#                    routes do not exist (404) -> RED.
#   (SERVE)          GET /rpm/{key}/@N/repodata/{repomd.xml,.asc,.key} -> 200,
#                    repomd indexes primary/filelists/other, .asc is PGP armor,
#                    .key is an OpenPGP public-key block.
#   (SIG-REAL)       the served .asc carries a CRC24 armor line AND (when gpg is
#                    present) gpg --verify of the .asc over the served repomd.xml
#                    under the imported .key SUCCEEDS. Signature is not theater.
#   (IMMUTABLE-PUB)  a second publish of the same version -> 409 Conflict.
#   (IMMUTABLE-META) approve a NEW package into the SAME staging repo AFTER
#                    publish, WITHOUT cutting/publishing a new version; re-GET
#                    @N/repodata/primary.xml.gz -> byte-identical to the first
#                    fetch. A live-regenerating serve path would leak the new
#                    package into the published snapshot -> RED.
#   (FROZEN-IDENTITY) the supply-chain gate. `curation_packages` is a LIVE table
#                    (a routine re-sync upserts the same row). After publishing
#                    @N, rewrite the live row's checksum + upstream path to point
#                    at attacker bytes, exactly as a poisoned re-sync would. GET
#                    @N/packages/{nevra}.rpm must NEVER serve the mutated bytes:
#                    it serves the ORIGINAL frozen bytes or fails closed with an
#                    integrity error, and any X-Checksum-SHA256 it advertises is
#                    the FROZEN hash, never the evil one. A serve path that
#                    resolves checksum/location from the live row is RED here.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login plus ~20
# back-to-back API + repodata calls; the base default (ON) would throttle them.
#
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the FROZEN-IDENTITY gate needs a real HTTP
# origin for the @N package fetch, so the oracle runs a throwaway nginx on the
# DTF docker network. It is only reachable at a private address, which the
# outbound SSRF guard rejects by default.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
